//RS /// c727 C1–C4: discover every top-level example, classify it, and ratchet the
//RS /// manifest. AOT-oracle examples (exit 0) must resident-JIT or deopt-interp
//RS /// with backend attribution — never silent fallback. Each AOT-oracle case
//RS /// compares default tiered against optimized AOT stdout/stderr/exit
//RS /// byte-for-byte, and compares the pure interpreter too WHEN the canonical MIR
//RS /// evaluator runs the program. A pure-interpreter refusal carrying E0956 is
//RS /// accepted and records `interpreter_refused: CODE` on the observed backend row. A
//RS /// `resident_jit` row proves AOT==tiered-JIT plus resident Cranelift execution.
//RS /// It proves interpreter parity only without that marker. Every accepted refusal
//RS /// is MIR coverage owed against D-ONECORE1=A/I9, not a settled boundary. Do not
//RS /// cite a marked `resident_jit` row as three-tier parity. An AOT-green example whose
//RS /// default tiered run REFUSES to run it lands in `run_tier_broken`, which must
//RS /// hold exactly `RUN_TIER_BROKEN_HELD_OUT`; one that runs but disagrees with the
//RS /// oracle lands in `tier_divergent`, which must stay empty. Recording both facts
//RS /// in one section made the run-tier message false for the divergences
//RS /// (D-VERDICT-1254-1 / D-LENS-RUN1). An example whose AOT oracle failed to build
//RS /// or exited non-zero gets no comparison at all and lands in `aot_broken`, which
//RS /// must hold exactly `AOT_BROKEN_HELD_OUT`: that fact used to be filed under
